<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">American Colonization Society</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1816">1816</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The American Colonization Society was an organisation founded in Washington in 1816 to settle free Black Americans in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. Its supporters ranged from opponents of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> to slaveholders who wished to remove free Black people from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and it was opposed by many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> and abolitionists. It founded the colony of Liberia in 1822 and helped several thousand settlers emigrate there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/american-colonization-society.jpg" alt="American Colonization Society" width="320" height="333" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Felipe Fidelis Tobias · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emblem_of_American_Colonization_Society.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The society began in 1816, when Charles Fenton Mercer, a Federalist member of the Virginia General Assembly, found accounts of earlier legislative debates on Black colonisation after Gabriel Prosser's rebellion. His contact John Caldwell approached his brother-in-law, the Presbyterian minister Robert Finley, who endorsed the plan. It was first called the Society for the Colonization of Free People of Color of America. Its members held that free people of colour could not integrate into American society. The population of free Black Americans had grown from 60,000 in 1790 to 300,000 by 1830, and slave owners feared that they might help enslaved people escape or organise rebellions.</p><p>The society drew on the example of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/committee-for-the-relief-of-the-black-poor">Committee for the Relief of the Black Poor</a>, which in 1786 began a colony in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a>, and of Paul Cuffe, a Quaker ship owner in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> who took 38 Black Americans to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freetown">Freetown</a> in 1816. Its first president was Bushrod Washington, a Supreme Court justice, and Henry Clay of Kentucky, a slaveholder, led it from 1836 to 1849.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The society raised money by selling memberships and by lobbying Congress, which gave it $100,000 in 1819. On 6 February 1820 the Elizabeth sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> with three white agents and 86 Black emigrants. In 1821 Lt. Robert Stockton pressed King Peter to sell Cape Montserrado, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monrovia">Monrovia</a> was founded, and in 1825 and 1826 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehudi-ashmun">Jehudi Ashmun</a> leased, annexed or bought more coastal land. Of 4,571 emigrants who arrived in Liberia between 1820 and 1843, only 1,819 were alive in 1843, and the society knew of the death rate but kept sending people. From 1825 it published the African Repository.</p><p>Most Black Americans opposed the plan. James Forten wrote in 1817 that they had no wish to separate from their homes, and 3,000 people met in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> church to denounce it. From the 1830s abolitionists led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-lloyd-garrison">William Lloyd Garrison</a>, whose Thoughts on African Colonization appeared in 1832, called it a fraud that helped preserve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. By 1833 it had moved only 2,769 people, and some were pressured to go.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The society continued during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>, when it sent 168 Black people to Liberia, and 2,492 in the five years after. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a> tried repeatedly to arrange resettlement of this kind early in his presidency, and each attempt failed. It essentially ended after 1919 but was formally dissolved only in 1964, when it gave its papers to the Library of Congress. Historians have judged it differently: some have seen it as a proslavery body, others, more recently, as an antislavery one, while many examine its racist rhetoric. Marc Leepson called colonisation a giant failure.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “American Colonization Society” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Liberia grew out of the work of the <b>American Colonization Society</b>, founded in 1816 by a coalition of white philanthropists, clergy and slaveholders who wished to resettle free African Americans in Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liberia">Liberia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Some white reformers backed the <b>American Colonization Society</b>, founded in 1816 to settle free Black Americans in Africa, but most Black abolitionists rejected it.</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>Black Christians took heart from the verse of the Psalms “Ethiopia shall soon stretch out her hands unto God”, and some settled in Sierra Leone, where Freetown was founded in 1792, or in Liberia, colonised from 1822 by the <b>American Colonization Society</b> and independent from 1847.</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Liberia, founded by the <b>American Colonization Society</b> as a home for freed Black Americans, declared itself an independent republic.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-26">26 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>American Colonization Society</b>, founded in 1816, aimed to settle free Black Americans in Africa.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-liberia">Colony of Liberia</a></li></ul></section>
